efinements\n\n*   **Real-Time Ingestion Layer:** To meet the sub-millisecond requirements of driver-to-customer tracking, we move away from Kafka for position updates. We will deploy a dedicated **WebSocket/gRPC Streaming Layer** (utilizing Envoy with gRPC streaming). This layer terminates real-time location data directly, bypassing broker latency, while asynchronously persisting data to **TimescaleDB** for historical analysis.\n*   **Distributed Transaction Management (Sagas):** To prevent state corruption, we will implement the **Saga Pattern (Choreography-based)**. Each microservice involved in the checkout flow will listen for \"Failure Events\" (e.g., `PaymentFailed`) and execute corresponding **Compensating Transactions** (e.g., `UpdateOrderToCancelled`) to ensure eventual consistency.\n*   **Security Hardening:** Beyond TLS/JWT, we are introducing:\n    *   **Application-Layer Rate Limiting:** Using a distributed token-bucket algorithm (via Redis) to prevent menu scraping and brute-force attacks on promotional codes.\n    *   **Zero-Trust Service Communication:** Leveraging Istio\u2019s mTLS for **RBAC/ABAC** policies, ensuring the `Notification` service has zero network access to the `Payment` service.\n*   **Zero-Downtime Database Migration:** To migrate to a sharded Citus environment, we will employ **Change Data Capture (CDC) using Debezium**. This allows for real-time synchronization between the legacy monolithic database and the new sharded nodes, enabling a cutover once verification is complete.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Quantitative & Qualitative Insights\n\n| Feature | Performance Impact | Complexity Level |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Saga (Choreography)** | High (Ensures Consistency) | High |\n| **gRPC/WebSockets** | Low Latency (Real-time tracking) | Moderate |\n| **Debezium/CDC** | Zero-downtime migration | High |\n| **Redis Rate Limiting** | Protects downstream stability | Low |\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommendations & Gap Analysis\n\n#### Recommendations:\n1